1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,742
Look up on a clear night,
far from the lights of any city,

2
00:00:09,822 --> 00:00:14,213
and you will see a pale band of
light arching across the sky.

3
00:00:14,293 --> 00:00:18,489
That band is the Milky Way,
the galaxy we call home.

4
00:00:18,569 --> 00:00:23,721
It is a great spiral of stars, gas,
and dust, held together by gravity,

5
00:00:23,801 --> 00:00:27,560
about one hundred thousand
light-years across.

6
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:33,789
Somewhere between one hundred billion and
four hundred billion stars live inside it.

7
00:00:33,869 --> 00:00:39,101
Our Sun is only one of them, tucked into
a quiet spur between two spiral arms,

8
00:00:39,181 --> 00:00:43,229
about twenty-six thousand
light-years from the center.

9
00:00:43,308 --> 00:00:46,229
From there, we circle
the galaxy once every

10
00:00:46,309 --> 00:00:49,680
two hundred and thirty
million years or so.

11
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:55,197
For most of human history, the Milky
Way seemed to be the entire universe.

12
00:00:55,277 --> 00:00:59,312
It is not. And one of its
neighbors is heading our way.

13
00:01:01,662 --> 00:01:05,707
In the autumn sky, in the
constellation Andromeda, a faint,

14
00:01:05,787 --> 00:01:09,793
fuzzy patch of light can
be seen with the naked eye.

15
00:01:09,873 --> 00:01:15,935
For a long time, astronomers thought it
was a cloud of gas inside our own galaxy.

16
00:01:16,015 --> 00:01:19,260
In the nineteen twenties,
Edwin Hubble measured its

17
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:23,034
distance and showed that
it is a galaxy of its own.

18
00:01:23,114 --> 00:01:25,564
Today we call it the Andromeda Galaxy,

19
00:01:25,644 --> 00:01:29,877
and it lies about two and a
half million light-years away.

20
00:01:29,957 --> 00:01:33,208
The light reaching your
eyes left it two and a half

21
00:01:33,288 --> 00:01:36,990
million years ago,
long before our species existed.

22
00:01:37,070 --> 00:01:42,150
Andromeda is the largest galaxy in our
local neighborhood of the universe,

23
00:01:42,230 --> 00:01:45,319
an estimated one trillion stars strong.

24
00:01:45,399 --> 00:01:50,869
In twenty fifteen, NASA's Hubble Space
Telescope captured a giant panorama of

25
00:01:50,949 --> 00:01:56,942
Andromeda in which more than one hundred
million individual stars can be seen.

26
00:01:57,022 --> 00:02:00,958
Two enormous spirals,
separated by a gulf of nearly

27
00:02:01,038 --> 00:02:05,181
empty space. And that
gulf is slowly closing.

28
00:02:07,031 --> 00:02:11,311
How do we know that Andromeda
is moving? We read its light.

29
00:02:11,391 --> 00:02:13,403
Light from something moving away is

30
00:02:13,483 --> 00:02:16,541
stretched toward the
red end of the spectrum.

31
00:02:16,621 --> 00:02:20,671
Light from something moving closer
is squeezed toward the blue.

32
00:02:20,751 --> 00:02:23,896
Almost every distant
galaxy shows a redshift,

33
00:02:23,976 --> 00:02:29,003
because space itself is expanding
and carrying them away from us.

34
00:02:29,083 --> 00:02:32,470
Andromeda is a rare
exception. In nineteen twelve,

35
00:02:32,550 --> 00:02:38,258
the astronomer Vesto Slipher found that
its light is shifted toward the blue.

36
00:02:38,338 --> 00:02:41,106
Andromeda is coming toward us.

37
00:02:41,256 --> 00:02:42,864
Relative to the Milky Way,

38
00:02:42,944 --> 00:02:47,663
it approaches at roughly one hundred
and ten kilometers per second.

39
00:02:47,743 --> 00:02:50,239
That is fast enough to cross the distance

40
00:02:50,319 --> 00:02:53,455
from the Earth to the
Moon in about an hour.

41
00:02:53,535 --> 00:02:57,560
And yet the gap between the
two galaxies is so vast that,

42
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:02,273
even at that speed,
the journey takes billions of years.

43
00:03:04,123 --> 00:03:08,426
Light tells us how fast a galaxy
moves along our line of sight.

44
00:03:08,506 --> 00:03:11,333
But it says nothing about sideways motion,

45
00:03:11,413 --> 00:03:17,250
and sideways motion decides whether two
galaxies collide or pass each other by.

46
00:03:17,330 --> 00:03:19,936
To measure it, astronomers have to watch

47
00:03:20,016 --> 00:03:23,342
the stars of Andromeda
drift across the sky.

48
00:03:23,422 --> 00:03:27,393
The shift is astonishingly
small, a tiny fraction of a

49
00:03:27,473 --> 00:03:32,344
single pixel in a Hubble image,
even after years of waiting.

50
00:03:32,424 --> 00:03:36,889
In twenty twelve, astronomers
compared Hubble images of Andromeda

51
00:03:36,969 --> 00:03:41,745
taken years apart, and measured
that motion for the first time.

52
00:03:41,825 --> 00:03:47,588
The result was clear: Andromeda is moving
almost directly toward the Milky Way.

53
00:03:47,668 --> 00:03:52,582
NASA announced that the two galaxies
were likely to collide in about four

54
00:03:52,662 --> 00:03:57,890
billion years, and to merge into one
around six billion years from now.

55
00:03:57,970 --> 00:04:01,387
Newer measurements,
including data from the European

56
00:04:01,467 --> 00:04:05,469
Space Agency's Gaia mission,
have refined the picture.

57
00:04:05,549 --> 00:04:07,764
And a twenty twenty five study,

58
00:04:07,844 --> 00:04:11,393
which included the pull
of other nearby galaxies,

59
00:04:11,473 --> 00:04:14,355
suggests the odds of a merger within ten

60
00:04:14,435 --> 00:04:17,989
billion years may be
closer to fifty-fifty.

61
00:04:18,069 --> 00:04:23,456
So the collision is not a certainty.
Science is still working on the answer.

62
00:04:25,806 --> 00:04:28,750
A collision between two
galaxies sounds like a

63
00:04:28,830 --> 00:04:32,554
cosmic catastrophe. It will
be nothing of the sort.

64
00:04:32,634 --> 00:04:36,522
Galaxies are mostly empty
space, and the stars inside

65
00:04:36,602 --> 00:04:41,240
them are separated by distances
that are hard to imagine.

66
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:43,463
Shrink the Sun to the size of a pea,

67
00:04:43,543 --> 00:04:47,864
and the nearest star would be
about two hundred kilometers away.

68
00:04:47,944 --> 00:04:50,459
When two galaxies pass through each other,

69
00:04:50,539 --> 00:04:55,729
their stars slip by like two swarms of
fireflies drifting through one another.

70
00:04:55,809 --> 00:04:58,564
The chance that any
two stars will actually

71
00:04:58,644 --> 00:05:01,388
hit each other is vanishingly small.

72
00:05:01,468 --> 00:05:05,213
But gravity needs no contact.
As the galaxies draw near,

73
00:05:05,293 --> 00:05:08,326
their pull will stretch and twist them.

74
00:05:08,406 --> 00:05:14,127
Long streams of stars, called tidal tails,
will be flung far out into space.

75
00:05:14,207 --> 00:05:19,500
Gas is different. Vast clouds of gas
and dust will crash into each other,

76
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:22,746
compress, and collapse into new stars.

77
00:05:22,826 --> 00:05:27,176
The calm spirals will become
something wild, and beautiful.

78
00:05:29,026 --> 00:05:31,582
We know what is coming
because we can watch

79
00:05:31,662 --> 00:05:34,423
it happening elsewhere in the universe.

80
00:05:34,503 --> 00:05:36,739
Tens of millions of light-years away,

81
00:05:36,819 --> 00:05:41,320
two galaxies called the Antennae
are in the middle of a collision.

82
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:45,720
Hubble, Chandra, and Spitzer
have photographed them in detail.

83
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:49,072
Their gas has ignited a
burst of star formation,

84
00:05:49,152 --> 00:05:54,479
and a swarm of brilliant young star
clusters now glows in the wreckage.

85
00:05:54,559 --> 00:05:59,003
In fact, the Milky Way itself
is a product of collisions.

86
00:05:59,083 --> 00:06:03,407
Astronomers using Gaia found
the remains of a dwarf galaxy

87
00:06:03,487 --> 00:06:07,806
that merged with ours roughly
ten billion years ago.

88
00:06:07,886 --> 00:06:10,922
Right now, a small
galaxy called Sagittarius

89
00:06:11,002 --> 00:06:13,401
is being torn apart by our gravity,

90
00:06:13,481 --> 00:06:18,738
its stars stretched into a long
stream wrapping around the Milky Way.

91
00:06:18,818 --> 00:06:24,085
Our galaxy has spent most of its life
growing by consuming its neighbors.

92
00:06:24,165 --> 00:06:28,712
The meeting with Andromeda would
simply be the biggest one yet.

93
00:06:30,562 --> 00:06:35,750
At the center of every large galaxy
lies a supermassive black hole.

94
00:06:35,830 --> 00:06:41,847
Ours, Sagittarius A-star, weighs about
four million times the mass of the Sun.

95
00:06:41,927 --> 00:06:47,579
Andromeda's is far heavier, at more
than one hundred million solar masses.

96
00:06:47,659 --> 00:06:52,859
As the galaxies merge, the two black
holes will slowly sink toward the middle

97
00:06:52,939 --> 00:06:58,452
of the new galaxy, dragged by the
gravity of the stars and gas around them.

98
00:06:58,532 --> 00:07:04,146
They will begin to orbit each other,
closer and closer, over millions of years.

99
00:07:04,226 --> 00:07:07,262
Orbiting black holes
shake spacetime itself,

100
00:07:07,342 --> 00:07:10,236
sending out gravitational waves that carry

101
00:07:10,316 --> 00:07:13,802
energy away and bring
the pair closer still.

102
00:07:13,882 --> 00:07:18,863
In the end, the two will merge into
a single, even larger black hole.

103
00:07:18,943 --> 00:07:21,694
Gas stirred up by the
collision may feed it,

104
00:07:21,774 --> 00:07:26,068
making the heart of the new
galaxy blaze brightly for a time.

105
00:07:26,148 --> 00:07:31,063
Waves from mergers like this are
the target of future observatories,

106
00:07:31,143 --> 00:07:35,618
such as the planned LISA mission
of the European Space Agency,

107
00:07:35,698 --> 00:07:37,987
with NASA as a partner.

108
00:07:39,837 --> 00:07:44,212
What would this look like from Earth,
if anyone were here to see it?

109
00:07:44,412 --> 00:07:48,470
Scientists have worked it out,
using the Hubble measurements.

110
00:07:48,550 --> 00:07:52,125
Today, Andromeda is a faint
smudge, though if our eyes

111
00:07:52,205 --> 00:07:56,366
could see all of it, it would
span about six full Moons.

112
00:07:56,446 --> 00:08:01,525
In roughly three to four billion years,
it would begin to fill the sky.

113
00:08:01,605 --> 00:08:04,420
Its spiral arms would
grow brighter and more

114
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:07,765
distorted as the tides
of gravity warp them.

115
00:08:07,845 --> 00:08:11,284
Then, as the two galaxies
pass through each other,

116
00:08:11,364 --> 00:08:17,646
the night sky would fill with glowing gas,
newborn stars, and tangled lanes of dust.

117
00:08:17,726 --> 00:08:22,788
Over time the display would settle,
as the two systems blend into one.

118
00:08:22,868 --> 00:08:26,856
It would be the grandest sight
any world has ever offered.

119
00:08:28,706 --> 00:08:31,781
There is one comfort here,
and one warning.

120
00:08:31,861 --> 00:08:35,627
The comfort is that stars are
so far apart that the Solar

121
00:08:35,707 --> 00:08:39,788
System will almost certainly
survive the merger intact.

122
00:08:39,868 --> 00:08:44,554
Simulations suggest the Sun and
its planets would be carried along,

123
00:08:44,634 --> 00:08:49,913
probably to a new orbit farther from
the center of the merged galaxy.

124
00:08:49,993 --> 00:08:52,567
The warning is that the greatest threat to

125
00:08:52,647 --> 00:08:55,924
life on Earth is not
Andromeda. It is the Sun.

126
00:08:56,004 --> 00:09:01,601
The Sun is slowly growing brighter,
by about ten percent every billion years.

127
00:09:01,681 --> 00:09:07,632
In about a billion years, that extra heat
will begin to evaporate Earth's oceans.

128
00:09:07,712 --> 00:09:12,244
Long before Andromeda arrives,
Earth as we know it will be gone.

129
00:09:12,324 --> 00:09:17,271
And in about five billion years,
the Sun will swell into a red giant,

130
00:09:17,351 --> 00:09:20,198
and end its life as a white dwarf.

131
00:09:20,278 --> 00:09:23,103
If anyone is watching the great collision,

132
00:09:23,183 --> 00:09:28,487
they will be our very distant descendants,
or someone else entirely.

133
00:09:30,337 --> 00:09:36,372
When the dust finally settles, billions of
years later, the two spirals will be gone.

134
00:09:36,452 --> 00:09:39,487
In their place will
stand a single, enormous,

135
00:09:39,567 --> 00:09:43,259
smooth galaxy shaped like
a ball, an elliptical.

136
00:09:43,339 --> 00:09:46,637
Astronomers have already
given it a nickname:

137
00:09:46,717 --> 00:09:50,539
Milkomeda, a blend of
Milky Way and Andromeda.

138
00:09:50,619 --> 00:09:54,955
The smaller Triangulum galaxy,
the third largest in our group,

139
00:09:55,035 --> 00:09:58,111
may be pulled into the merger as well.

140
00:09:58,191 --> 00:10:04,133
The new galaxy will slowly run low on gas,
and the birth of new stars will fade.

141
00:10:04,213 --> 00:10:08,531
Far in the future, as the expansion
of the universe speeds up,

142
00:10:08,611 --> 00:10:12,812
almost every other galaxy
will drift beyond our reach.

143
00:10:12,892 --> 00:10:16,922
Only our own small family of
galaxies will stay together.

144
00:10:17,002 --> 00:10:21,528
For the first time, a species on
a small planet has measured the

145
00:10:21,608 --> 00:10:26,655
motion of a neighboring galaxy,
and read its own galaxy's future.

146
00:10:26,735 --> 00:10:30,924
The universe is not a still
picture. It is a story of motion,

147
00:10:31,004 --> 00:10:34,193
of collisions, and of new beginnings.
